{%- comment -%}
  The real Shopify contact form, hidden, for the Somm's ask-a-human card.

  WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A fetch(). The card first posted its own payload
  to /contact — correct fields, correct shape, matching the contact page's form
  exactly — and Shopify answered 400 every time, by fetch and by a native form
  in a hidden iframe alike. No email arrived. Verified by checking the inbox,
  not by trusting the status code.

  The cause is Shopify's spam protection: it injects invisible bot detection
  into forms rendered by the {% form %} TAG, and rejects contact posts that
  arrive without it. A hand-built form can never carry that token, so no amount
  of getting the fields right was ever going to work.

  So the card fills in this one and submits it. Shopify's own protection rides
  along because Shopify rendered it, and delivery is the same path the contact
  page already uses — which is the "no new credentials, no separate
  deliverability setup" the plan called for, arrived at properly.

  Hidden with a class rather than the `hidden` attribute: a hidden attribute on
  the wrapper would make the fields unfocusable, and Shopify's own protection
  watches for interaction.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="non-ask-form" aria-hidden="true">
  {%- form 'contact', id: 'non-ask-form', class: 'non-ask-form__form' -%}
    <input type="hidden" name="contact[name]" value="" data-non-ask-name>
    <input type="hidden" name="contact[email]" value="" data-non-ask-email>
    <input type="hidden" name="contact[Subject]" value="" data-non-ask-subject>
    <textarea name="contact[body]" data-non-ask-message></textarea>
  {%- endform -%}
</div>
